    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Independent from 1821, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> grew from the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> as its capital.</li><li>Guano wealth funded railways and reforms, but defeat by Chile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> cost it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a>.</li><li>After military rule, hyperinflation and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a> insurgency, it became a fast-growing mining economy with fragile politics.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>From the 1840s to the 1870s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s main export was guano, seabird droppings dug from offshore islands and shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as fertiliser.</p></aside>
    </section>

