    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii.jpg" alt="Túpac Amaru II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TupacAmaruII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s independence came late and largely from outside. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> was the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>’s oldest and richest South American viceroyalty, heir to the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> elite, shaken by the great rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a> in 1780–1781, stayed mostly loyal to the crown. In September 1820 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> landed an army from Chile at Paracas; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> abandoned Lima, and on 28 July 1821 San Martín proclaimed Peru’s independence in the capital and took the title of Protector. After his meeting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> in 1822 he withdrew, leaving a constituent congress that produced the first constitution in 1823 but could not defeat the royalists, who still held the highlands. Bolívar arrived in September 1823 and was granted dictatorial powers in 1824. His Colombian and Peruvian army won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-junin">Battle of Junín</a> in August 1824, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a> ended Spanish power at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ayacucho">Battle of Ayacucho</a> on 9 December 1824. The last Spanish garrison, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/real-felipe-fortress">Real Felipe</a> fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a>, surrendered in January 1826. Bolívar left Peru that year, and in 1827 congress repealed the life-presidency constitution he had imposed, beginning the republic’s independent political life.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lima"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lima.jpg" alt="Lima" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas%C3%ADlica_Catedral_Metropolitana_de_Lima_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David Felipe Ruiz Hoyos, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Independence left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> with a divided society: a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> elite in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and the coastal valleys, a largely indigenous, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a>- and Aymara-speaking majority in the Andes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afro-peruvians">Afro-Peruvians</a> on the coastal estates and mestizos in between. The colonial Indian tribute, renamed the <em>contribución de indígenas</em>, remained a main source of revenue until the guano boom. From the 1840s the state exported vast deposits of seabird guano, mainly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chincha-islands">Chincha Islands</a>, as fertiliser for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and guano revenue paid for the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and the tribute in 1854, for railways and a growing bureaucracy, but also for heavy borrowing. Roughly 100,000 Chinese indentured labourers were brought to the coastal plantations and guano islands between 1849 and 1874. Nitrates, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, wool and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> followed, and in the twentieth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, oil and fishmeal. In the highlands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> expanded at the expense of indigenous communities until the agrarian reform of 1969. Migration from the Andes transformed Lima from the 1940s, creating vast self-built settlements and a large informal economy. Since the 2000s mining, especially of copper, gold and silver, and agricultural exports have driven rapid growth and reduced poverty, though inequality between Lima and the provinces remains sharp. Peru is a largely Catholic country of about 34 million people.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yungay"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-yungay.jpg" alt="Battle of Yungay" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yungay">Battle of Yungay</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plano_de_la_Batalla_de_Yungay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For much of the nineteenth century the army was the arbiter of politics, and caudillos such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-gamarra">Agustín Gamarra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-castilla">Ramón Castilla</a> won the presidency by force. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Gran</a> Colombia in 1828–1829, and in 1836 the Bolivian president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-de-santa-cruz">Andrés de Santa Cruz</a> intervened in a Peruvian civil war to create the Peru-Bolivian Confederation, which Chile and Peruvian exiles destroyed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yungay">Battle of Yungay</a> in January 1839. Gamarra was killed invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ingavi">Battle of Ingavi</a> in 1841. Guano wealth bought a modern navy, and in 1866 Peru, allied with Chile against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, repelled a Spanish squadron at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-callao">Battle of Callao</a>. The great disaster was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> of 1879–1883, into which Peru was drawn by its secret alliance with Bolivia. Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-grau-seminario">Miguel Grau</a>’s ironclad <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a></em> raided the Chilean coast until it was captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-angamos">Battle of Angamos</a> in October 1879, where Grau was killed. Chile took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a> and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in January 1881, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-avelino-caceres">Andrés Avelino Cáceres</a> led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> resistance in the central highlands. In the twentieth century Peru fought Colombia over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia-peru-war">Leticia</a> in 1932–1933 and Ecuador in 1941, 1981 and 1995, and from 1980 to 2000 the armed forces waged a brutal counter-insurgency against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ramon-castilla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramon-castilla.jpg" alt="Ramón Castilla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ramon-castilla">Ramón Castilla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RamonCastilla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> has had twelve constitutions, and its history has alternated between elected governments and military rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-castilla">Castilla</a>’s constitution of 1860 lasted until 1920 and gave the country its first long institutional stability. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-pardo">Manuel Pardo</a>, elected in 1872 as candidate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civilista-party">Civilista Party</a>, became the first elected civilian president, and between 1895 and 1919 the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristocratic-republic">Aristocratic Republic</a> of coastal landowners and financiers governed through regular elections with a narrow, literate electorate. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-b-leguia">Augusto B. Leguía</a> ruled for eleven years from 1919, centralising power and borrowing heavily in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Depression</a> brought him down in 1930. Politics in the following decades turned on the conflict between the oligarchy, the army and the mass party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">APRA</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-raul-haya-de-la-torre">Víctor Raúl Haya de la Torre</a> in 1924, which was repeatedly banned. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-velasco-alvarado">Juan Velasco Alvarado</a> seized power in 1968 and carried out a radical programme: nationalisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-petroleum-company">International Petroleum Company</a>, a sweeping agrarian reform and official recognition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a>. The 1979 constitution restored civilian rule and extended the vote to the illiterate. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberto-fujimori">Alberto Fujimori</a>, elected in 1990, closed Congress in his self-coup of April 1992 and introduced the 1993 constitution, still in force, which strengthened the presidency and created a single-chamber Congress; a return to two chambers was approved in 2024.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tradiciones-peruanas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tradiciones-peruanas.jpg" alt="Tradiciones peruanas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tradiciones-peruanas">Tradiciones peruanas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tradiciones_peruanas_1893.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ricardo Palma, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As heir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> and colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, the Peruvian republic carries one of the deepest cultural legacies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Nineteenth-century writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ricardo-palma">Ricardo Palma</a>, author of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tradiciones-peruanas">Tradiciones peruanas</a></em>, shaped a national memory, and in the twentieth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-carlos-mariategui">José Carlos Mariátegui</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-interpretive-essays-on-peruvian-reality">Seven Interpretive Essays on Peruvian Reality</a></em> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American Marxist thought, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-raul-haya-de-la-torre">Haya de la Torre</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-popular-revolutionary-alliance">APRA</a> inspired populist parties across the continent. The poetry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesar-vallejo">César Vallejo</a> and the novels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-arguedas">José María Arguedas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mario-vargas-llosa">Mario Vargas Llosa</a>, who won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 2010, gave Peruvian literature a world audience, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavo-gutierrez">Gustavo Gutiérrez</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-theology-of-liberation">A Theology of Liberation</a></em> of 1971 influenced Catholic thinking far beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiram-bingham-iii">Hiram Bingham</a>’s publicising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a> after 1911 and later archaeology made Peru a symbol of Andean civilisation and a major tourist destination. The guano age tied the country closely to British finance and world agriculture, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> fixed borders and grievances that still shape relations with Chile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javier-perez-de-cuellar">Javier Pérez de Cuéllar</a> served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-general-of-the-united-nations">Secretary-General of the United Nations</a> from 1982 to 1991. More recently Peruvian cuisine has won international recognition, and the port of Chancay, built with Chinese investment and opened in 2024, has made Peru a growing hub for trans-Pacific trade.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ayacucho"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ayacucho.jpg" alt="Battle of Ayacucho" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ayacucho">Battle of Ayacucho</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_Ayacucho_by_Mart%C3%ADn_Tovar_y_Tovar_(1827_-_1902).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martín Tovar y Tovar, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The modern Peruvian state was shaped above all by the crises of the late twentieth century. The military government handed power back in 1980, but on the eve of the elections the Maoist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abimael-guzman">Abimael Guzmán</a>, began an armed struggle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ayacucho">Ayacucho</a>. The conflict that followed, involving the Shining Path, the smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-revolutionary-movement">Túpac Amaru Revolutionary Movement</a> and the security forces, cost about 69,000 lives according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-peru">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a> of 2003, most of them Quechua-speaking peasants. At the same time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-garcia">Alan García</a>’s first government ended in hyperinflation. Fujimori’s shock stabilisation, privatisations and the capture of Guzmán in September 1992 restored order, but his rule grew authoritarian and corrupt, and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 2000 amid a bribery scandal; he was later convicted of human-rights abuses. Since 2001 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> has held regular elections and enjoyed one of the fastest-growing economies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, with free-trade agreements with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and membership of the Pacific Alliance. Politics, however, has been unstable: several recent presidents have faced prosecution, and conflict between presidents and Congress has produced frequent removals, including that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-castillo">Pedro Castillo</a> after his failed attempt to dissolve Congress in December 2022 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dina-boluarte">Dina Boluarte</a> in October 2025. In 2026 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keiko-fujimori">Keiko Fujimori</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberto-fujimori">Alberto Fujimori</a>’s daughter, narrowly won the presidency. Peru remains a unitary presidential republic with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> as its capital.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, the republic that inherited the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>’s richest South American viceroyalty, has passed through a guano boom, defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a>, military reformism and a devastating insurgency, and remains a major mining economy and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>’s great cultural centres.</em></p>

